8
00:00:23.727 --> 00:00:29.081
What you will build: a repeatable way to run a 45 to 60 minute system design interview.

9
00:00:29.361 --> 00:00:40.263
It covers what to do in which minute, which questions to ask first, how precise the numbers need to be, how to defend a choice when the interviewer pushes, and what the rubric is really scoring.

10
00:00:40.543 --> 00:00:43.209
The previous 56 chapters are the content.

11
00:00:43.489 --> 00:00:48.695
This chapter is how to deliver it under a clock, to a stranger, at a 15-year level.

12
00:00:48.975 --> 00:00:50.233
What the labs measured:

13
00:00:50.513 --> 00:00:52.028
L1, estimation.

14
00:00:52.308 --> 00:00:53.951
Across 8 classic prompts:

15
00:00:55.301 --> 00:01:06.138
Rounding every input to 1-or-5 times 10ᵏ missed by up to 7.3 times and changed the implied architecture in 3 of 8 prompts.

16
00:01:06.418 --> 00:01:12.647
Keeping one significant figure stayed within 1.8 times and left 8 of 8 decisions unchanged.

17
00:01:12.927 --> 00:01:14.293
L2, trade-offs.

18
00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:18.242
A weighted decision matrix with 100,000 perturbations:

19
00:01:18.522 --> 00:01:23.471
Kafka for the event backbone won 87% of the time: defend it calmly.

20
00:01:23.751 --> 00:01:31.773
The cart store was a tie between PostgreSQL and a managed KV store: present it as a close call and name what flips it.

21
00:01:33.123 --> 00:01:35.192
L3, clarifying questions.

22
00:01:35.472 --> 00:01:39.803
Design a notification system has 48 plausible requirement sets:

23
00:01:40.083 --> 00:01:45.437
Designing for the typical reading fits 1 of them, and misses an essential component in 40.

24
00:01:45.717 --> 00:01:48.576
Asking just scale and latency leaves 0 missing.

25
00:01:48.856 --> 00:01:50.179
L4, the rubric.

26
00:01:50.459 --> 00:01:52.208
Three illustrative transcripts:

27
00:01:52.488 --> 00:01:55.005
The fast box-drawer scored 24%.

28
00:01:55.285 --> 00:02:00.725
A solid senior scored 79%, which passes a senior bar but not a staff one.

29
00:02:02.075 --> 00:02:08.326
The staff transcript scored 97%, using the same framework with every phase done deliberately.

30
