Part 1.

33
00:02:13.737 --> 00:02:15.657
Introduction and Why It Matters.

34
00:02:16.907 --> 00:02:19.510
A story: the architect who failed the interview.

35
00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:24.910
Ravi had led the platform team for six years and designed half the systems in this handbook.

36
00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:28.753
In a staff-level loop, he got design a notification system.

37
00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:35.091
Within ninety seconds he was drawing Kafka, three provider adapters, a preference service and a scheduler.

38
00:02:35.371 --> 00:02:37.483
It was his real system, beautifully.

39
00:02:37.763 --> 00:02:38.723
The feedback:

40
00:02:39.003 --> 00:02:40.689
Didn't clarify requirements.

41
00:02:40.969 --> 00:02:42.931
Over-engineered for the stated scale.

42
00:02:43.211 --> 00:02:48.289
When asked why Kafka over a database queue, repeated that Kafka is industry standard.

43
00:02:49.639 --> 00:02:52.135
Ran out of time before discussing failures.

44
00:02:52.415 --> 00:03:00.031
The interviewer had in mind 1,000 push notifications a second, best-effort, and wanted to see OTP latency handled.

45
00:03:00.311 --> 00:03:03.148
Experience is necessary, but not sufficient.

46
00:03:03.428 --> 00:03:11.385
An interview measures whether you can make your reasoning visible: find out which system is being asked for;. size it;. choose, and say why;.

47
00:03:12.735 --> 00:03:16.533
break it on purpose and fix it;. do all of that in 45 minutes.

48
00:03:16.813 --> 00:03:18.263
That is a skill of its own.

49
00:03:18.543 --> 00:03:19.909
This chapter trains it.

50
00:03:21.259 --> 00:03:21.941
Quote:

51
00:03:22.221 --> 00:03:23.885
The thesis of this chapter.

52
00:03:24.165 --> 00:03:25.509
Ask before you draw.

53
00:03:25.789 --> 00:03:32.339
The prompt describes many systems, and two questions (scale and latency) eliminate most of them (L3).

54
00:03:32.619 --> 00:03:37.696
Estimate to one significant figure and let the numbers pick the architecture (L1).

55
00:03:37.976 --> 00:03:44.717
Make trade-offs inspectable: know which choices are robust and which are close calls, and what would flip them (L2).

56
00:03:44.997 --> 00:03:51.205
Spend the clock on the deep dive and failures, because that's where senior and staff signals live (L4).

