8 of 8.

232
00:10:16.005 --> 00:10:16.923
Shortcuts:

233
00:10:17.203 --> 00:10:24.947
1 day about 10⁵ s; 1 month about 2.5 times 10⁶ s; 1 year about 3 times 10⁷ s.

234
00:10:25.227 --> 00:10:28.619
1M requests per day about 12 per second average.

235
00:10:28.899 --> 00:10:31.245
Peak = average times peak factor.

236
00:10:31.525 --> 00:10:36.304
Compute what decides: peak writes (can one primary take it?), peak reads (cache?

237
00:10:36.584 --> 00:10:42.771
CDN?), storage over retention (one machine? sharded?), bandwidth (egress cost, CDN).

238
00:10:43.051 --> 00:10:46.635
Thresholds to know (rules of thumb, VERIFY for your hardware):

239
00:10:47.985 --> 00:11:02.534
~5k writes per second for a comfortable single PostgreSQL primary;. ~100k ops per second per Redis instance;. ~1 GB per second per NIC;. ~10 TB per machine.

240
00:11:02.814 --> 00:11:07.891
Say near a threshold out loud, and design for the higher side (Ch 02).

241
00:11:09.241 --> 00:11:10.457
Steps 3 to 5:

242
00:11:10.737 --> 00:11:13.340
API, data model, high-level design.

243
00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:20.532
API: resource-oriented or RPC, idempotency keys on mutations, pagination, versioning.

244
00:11:20.812 --> 00:11:23.521
Events for async flows (Ch 04).

245
00:11:23.801 --> 00:11:39.247
Data model: start from access patterns, then choose stores per pattern (relational for money and inventory, KV for carts and sessions, search engine for text, object storage for media) (Ch 05, Ch 06).

246
00:11:39.527 --> 00:11:45.820
High-level design: clients, then edge, then services, then stores, with async via a queue or log.

247
00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:51.049
Name the critical path, and mark sync versus async edges (Ch 56).

248
00:11:52.399 --> 00:11:53.743
Keep it simple first.

249
00:11:54.023 --> 00:12:03.559
Add components only when a requirement or number demands them, and say which one (reads are 100 times writes at 40k per second, so a cache).

