00:26:58.133 --> 00:26:59.371
'write throughput', then.

517
00:26:59.651 --> 00:27:05.603
Managed KV (DynamoDB-like); 'ops burden', then Managed KV (DynamoDB-like).

518
00:27:05.883 --> 00:27:09.168
Event backbone (order events, replay, 20 consumers).

519
00:27:09.448 --> 00:27:13.523
Kafka base score 81.0 ← base-case winner.

520
00:27:13.803 --> 00:27:16.683
RabbitMQ base score 55.0.

521
00:27:16.963 --> 00:27:20.312
SQS or SNS base score 49.0.

522
00:27:20.592 --> 00:27:22.107
The rest is in the chapter.

523
00:27:24.957 --> 00:27:25.810
Reading it:

524
00:27:26.090 --> 00:27:29.567
Kafka and managed search win under most reasonable weightings.

525
00:27:29.847 --> 00:27:31.085
Defend them calmly.

526
00:27:31.365 --> 00:27:32.794
The cart store is a tie.

527
00:27:33.074 --> 00:27:35.677
Present it as a close call and name what flips it.

528
00:27:35.957 --> 00:27:37.898
Search: the matrix picks SaaS.

529
00:27:38.178 --> 00:27:47.202
The handbook chose self-run OpenSearch, which is a prompt to name the criterion the matrix is missing (control, cost at scale, residency).

530
00:27:48.552 --> 00:27:50.877
L3, The value of the first questions.

531
00:27:51.157 --> 00:27:52.096
Requirement:

532
00:27:52.376 --> 00:27:55.789
Design the system the interviewer means, not the one in your head.

533
00:27:56.069 --> 00:28:01.147
Every plausible reading of design a notification system, and the design each needs.

534
00:28:02.497 --> 00:28:05.590
Here is the Java code, ClarifyL3.java.

535
00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:09.518
It runs to 98 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

536
00:28:09.798 --> 00:28:17.350
ClarifyL3.java, run with: java ClarifyL3.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

537
00:28:17.630 --> 00:28:19.678
WHY THE FIRST FIVE MINUTES ARE QUESTIONS.

538
00:28:19.958 --> 00:28:20.726
Prompt:

539
00:28:21.006 --> 00:28:22.969
Design a notification system.

540
00:28:23.249 --> 00:28:31.526
Five things the prompt doesn't. say, each with plausible answers (every combination is a requirement set an interviewer might have in mind):

541
00:28:34.376 --> 00:28:35.293
On screen:

542
00:28:35.573 --> 00:28:44.661
48 plausible requirement sets for design a notification system; each implies an. architecture. distinct architectures implied:

543
00:28:44.941 --> 00:29:05.955
48 (uncertainty 5.58 bits). information gain per question: scale 1.58 bits latency 1.00 bits guarantee 1.00. bits channels 1.00 bits preferences 1.00 bits. strategy design right for over-built missing a need.

544
00:29:06.235 --> 00:29:11.269
Q0) no questions: assume the typical set 1 of 48 7 40.

545
00:29:11.549 --> 00:29:17.608
Q1) ask the 1 most informative question (scale) 3 of 48 21 24.

546
00:29:17.888 --> 00:29:24.373
Q2) ask the 2 most informative questions (scale, latency) 6 of 48 42 0.

547
00:29:24.653 --> 00:29:28.280
Q5) ask all five 48 of 48 0 0.

548
00:29:28.560 --> 00:29:30.075
The rest is in the chapter.

549
00:29:32.925 --> 00:29:33.778
Reading it:

550
00:29:34.058 --> 00:29:39.391
Without questions, the design misses an essential component in 40 of 48 readings.

551
00:29:39.671 --> 00:29:43.042
Asking scale, then latency removes every missing case.

552
00:29:43.322 --> 00:29:47.034
Stating the remaining assumptions aloud removes the over-building.

553
00:29:48.384 --> 00:29:50.432
L4, What the rubric rewards.

554
00:29:50.712 --> 00:29:51.651
Requirement:

555
00:29:51.931 --> 00:29:54.640
Know which behaviours move the score, at your level.

556
00:29:54.920 --> 00:29:58.909
Three illustrative transcripts scored against senior and staff rubrics.

557
00:30:00.259 --> 00:30:03.267
Here is the Java code, RubricL4.java.

558
00:30:03.547 --> 00:30:07.238
It runs to 78 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

559
00:30:07.518 --> 00:30:14.665
RubricL4.java, run with: java RubricL4.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

560
00:30:14.945 --> 00:30:17.654
HOW A 45-MINUTE DESIGN INTERVIEW IS SCORED.

561
00:30:17.934 --> 00:30:27.662
A rubric of 8 signals (the ones this chapter's framework is built to. produce), each needing specific evidence, weighted by level, plus time discipline.

562
00:30:27.942 --> 00:30:29.841
Three ILLUSTRATIVE transcripts of.

563
00:30:32.691 --> 00:30:38.387
On screen: transcript (illustrative) senior staff missing or weak signals time discipline.

564
00:30:38.667 --> 00:30:41.568
(bar 70%) (bar 85%).

565
00:30:41.848 --> 00:30:53.517
A, draws boxes fast 24% no 20% no requirements (absent), estimation (absent). api+data model (weak) +4 designed before asking.

566
00:30:53.797 --> 00:31:00.859
B, solid senior 79% hire 75% no operability or cost (weak) on plan.

567
00:31:01.139 --> 00:31:13.853
C, staff signals 96% hire 97% hire, on plan. bars (illustrative): senior ≥ 70%, staff ≥ 85%.

568
00:31:14.133 --> 00:31:30.133
The two rubrics also weigh the same. evidence differently: staff puts more weight on trade-offs, failure handling and operability, less on API. detail. then the fast box-drawer loses on everything the boxes don't show: no requirements, no.

569
00:31:30.413 --> 00:31:31.928
The rest is in the chapter.

570
00:31:34.778 --> 00:31:35.631
Reading it:

571
00:31:35.911 --> 00:31:40.263
Speed without requirements, numbers or failures scores poorly at any level.

572
00:31:40.543 --> 00:31:46.218
The senior-to-staff gap is trade-offs, failure handling and operability done as first-class work.

573
00:31:46.498 --> 00:31:48.717
The transcripts and bars are illustrative.

574
00:31:50.067 --> 00:31:51.005
Part 6.

575
00:31:51.285 --> 00:31:52.672
Interview-Day Concerns.

576
00:31:53.922 --> 00:31:55.074
Failure modes.

577
00:31:55.354 --> 00:31:56.719
Here is a quick table:

578
00:31:56.999 --> 00:31:58.855
Failure, and Symptom, and Fix.

579
00:31:59.135 --> 00:32:00.565
Designing before asking.

580
00:32:00.845 --> 00:32:01.655
Symptom:

581
00:32:01.935 --> 00:32:03.493
Wrong system (L3).

582
00:32:03.773 --> 00:32:04.498
Fix:

583
00:32:04.778 --> 00:32:06.933
Two questions first, then assumptions.

584
00:32:07.213 --> 00:32:08.322
Coarse estimates.

585
00:32:08.602 --> 00:32:09.413
Symptom:

586
00:32:09.693 --> 00:32:11.314
Wrong decisions (L1).

587
00:32:11.594 --> 00:32:12.319
Fix:

588
00:32:12.599 --> 00:32:15.095
One significant figure, name the thresholds.

589
00:32:15.375 --> 00:32:16.677
No numbers at all.

590
00:32:16.957 --> 00:32:17.767
Symptom:

591
00:32:18.047 --> 00:32:19.413
Hand-wavy feedback.

592
00:32:19.693 --> 00:32:20.418
Fix:

593
00:32:20.698 --> 00:32:22.511
4 to 6 numbers on the board.

594
00:32:22.791 --> 00:32:23.879
Over-engineering.

595
00:32:24.159 --> 00:32:24.970
Symptom:

596
00:32:25.250 --> 00:32:26.381
Didn't fit the scale.

597
00:32:26.661 --> 00:32:27.386
Fix:

598
00:32:27.666 --> 00:32:30.162
Add components only when a number demands it.

599
00:32:30.442 --> 00:32:31.658
Shallow everywhere.

600
00:32:31.938 --> 00:32:32.749
Symptom:

601
00:32:33.029 --> 00:32:33.882
No depth.

602
00:32:34.162 --> 00:32:34.887
Fix:

603
00:32:35.167 --> 00:32:37.919
One real deep dive with code-level detail.

604
00:32:38.199 --> 00:32:39.330
Losing the clock.

605
00:32:39.610 --> 00:32:40.421
Symptom:

606
00:32:40.701 --> 00:32:42.749
No failure discussion (L4).

607
00:32:43.029 --> 00:32:43.754
Fix:

608
00:32:44.034 --> 00:32:45.293
Timebox out loud.

609
00:32:45.573 --> 00:32:47.343
Defending everything equally.

610
00:32:47.623 --> 00:32:48.434
Symptom:

611
00:32:48.714 --> 00:32:49.525
Rigid.

612
00:32:49.805 --> 00:32:50.530
Fix:

613
00:32:50.810 --> 00:32:53.605
Robust versus close-call trade-offs (L2).

614
00:32:54.955 --> 00:32:56.021
Ignoring hints.

615
00:32:56.301 --> 00:32:57.112
Symptom:

616
00:32:57.392 --> 00:32:58.331
Didn't listen.

617
00:32:58.611 --> 00:32:59.336
Fix:

618
00:32:59.616 --> 00:33:01.195
Treat questions as steering.

619
00:33:02.545 --> 00:33:03.825
Preparation plan.

620
00:33:04.105 --> 00:33:09.779
Weeks 1 to 2: the foundations and core chapters on your path (Ch 00 paths).

621
00:33:10.059 --> 00:33:16.225
Weeks 3 to 4: one case study a day, timed at 45 minutes on paper, before reading the chapter.

622
00:33:16.505 --> 00:33:22.606
Weeks 5 to 6: mock interviews (Ch 60) with the rubric (L4), recorded and reviewed.

623
00:33:23.956 --> 00:33:25.919
Whiteboard and virtual hygiene.

624
00:33:26.199 --> 00:33:33.068
Layout: requirements top-left, numbers top-right, diagram in the centre, decisions and failure tables below.

625
00:33:33.348 --> 00:33:36.655
Virtual: pre-agree on a tool (Excalidraw, a doc).

626
00:33:36.935 --> 00:33:39.004
Share early, and narrate what you draw.

627
00:33:40.354 --> 00:33:41.207
Pacing.

628
00:33:41.487 --> 00:33:46.287
Glance at the clock at minutes 5, 14, 20, 35 and 41.

629
00:33:46.567 --> 00:33:48.402
If you're behind, say what you'll skip.

630
00:33:48.682 --> 00:33:54.079
If the interviewer dives early, follow them, and return to the skipped steps briefly at the end.

631
00:33:55.429 --> 00:33:56.304
Mindset.

632
00:33:56.584 --> 00:33:58.376
The interviewer wants you to succeed.

633
00:33:58.656 --> 00:34:01.109
Treat it as a design review with a colleague.

634
00:34:01.389 --> 00:34:03.544
Say I don't know plus how you would find out.

635
00:34:03.824 --> 00:34:04.997
It beats bluffing.

636
00:34:06.347 --> 00:34:07.286
Part 7.

637
00:34:07.566 --> 00:34:09.273
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

638
00:34:10.523 --> 00:34:11.440
Watch out:

639
00:34:11.720 --> 00:34:12.360
1.

640
00:34:12.640 --> 00:34:14.112
Drawing in the first minute.

641
00:34:14.392 --> 00:34:17.059
Designing an unspecified system (L3).

642
00:34:18.409 --> 00:34:19.326
Watch out:

643
00:34:19.606 --> 00:34:20.310
2.

644
00:34:20.590 --> 00:34:22.105
Asking twenty questions.

645
00:34:22.385 --> 00:34:25.435
Ask the few that split the space, then state assumptions.

646
00:34:26.785 --> 00:34:27.703
Watch out:

647
00:34:27.983 --> 00:34:28.729
3.

648
00:34:29.009 --> 00:34:30.247
Arithmetic theatre.

649
00:34:30.527 --> 00:34:32.105
Precision without decisions.

650
00:34:33.455 --> 00:34:34.373
Watch out:

651
00:34:34.653 --> 00:34:35.357
4.

652
00:34:35.637 --> 00:34:37.365
Coarse rounding near thresholds.

653
00:34:37.645 --> 00:34:39.565
Flipped architectures (L1).

654
00:34:40.915 --> 00:34:41.832
Watch out:

655
00:34:42.112 --> 00:34:42.880
5.

656
00:34:43.160 --> 00:34:44.568
Buzzword architecture.

657
00:34:44.848 --> 00:34:49.541
Kafka, Kubernetes and microservices with no requirement behind them.

658
00:34:50.891 --> 00:34:51.809
Watch out:

659
00:34:52.089 --> 00:34:52.835
6.

660
00:34:53.115 --> 00:34:54.310
Never going deep.

661
00:34:54.590 --> 00:34:57.022
Senior interviews are won in the deep dive.

662
00:34:58.372 --> 00:34:59.289
Watch out:

663
00:34:59.569 --> 00:35:00.316
7.

664
00:35:00.596 --> 00:35:02.111
Ignoring failure handling.

665
00:35:02.391 --> 00:35:03.457
The staff signal.

666
00:35:04.807 --> 00:35:05.725
Watch out:

667
00:35:06.005 --> 00:35:06.687
8.

668
00:35:06.967 --> 00:35:09.549
'Industry standard' as a justification.

669
00:35:09.829 --> 00:35:12.154
Name requirements and costs (L2).

670
00:35:13.504 --> 00:35:14.421
Watch out:

671
00:35:14.701 --> 00:35:15.448
9.

672
00:35:15.728 --> 00:35:17.307
Arguing with the interviewer.

673
00:35:17.587 --> 00:35:19.997
Probes are invitations, not attacks.

674
00:35:21.347 --> 00:35:22.265
Watch out:

675
00:35:22.545 --> 00:35:23.249
10.

676
00:35:23.529 --> 00:35:24.531
Monologuing.

677
00:35:24.811 --> 00:35:26.390
Check in at step boundaries.

678
00:35:27.740 --> 00:35:28.657
Watch out:

679
00:35:28.937 --> 00:35:29.769
11.

680
00:35:30.049 --> 00:35:32.481
Recreating your employer's system verbatim.

681
00:35:32.761 --> 00:35:34.980
Fit the design to the stated requirements.

682
00:35:36.330 --> 00:35:37.247
Watch out:

683
00:35:37.527 --> 00:35:38.317
12.

684
00:35:38.597 --> 00:35:40.175
Running out of time silently.

685
00:35:40.455 --> 00:35:42.055
Say what you would cover next.

686
00:35:43.405 --> 00:35:44.280
Part 8.

687
00:35:44.560 --> 00:35:45.904
Interview Q and A.

688
00:35:47.154 --> 00:35:47.986
Beginner.

689
00:35:48.266 --> 00:35:49.290
Question 1.

690
00:35:49.570 --> 00:35:53.239
How do you structure a 45-minute system design interview?

691
00:35:53.519 --> 00:35:54.885
Thirty-second answer:

692
00:35:55.165 --> 00:36:12.317
Clarify (5 min), estimate (3), API and data model (6), high-level design (6), deep dive (15), failures and scaling (6), wrap-up (4), timeboxed out loud, with requirements and numbers on the board and check-ins at each transition.

693
00:36:13.667 --> 00:36:14.669
Question 2.

694
00:36:14.949 --> 00:36:16.741
Which questions do you ask first?

695
00:36:17.021 --> 00:36:18.387
Thirty-second answer:

696
00:36:18.667 --> 00:36:30.933
The ones that change the architecture most: scale (orders of magnitude) and latency or consistency needs, plus the core use cases; then state assumptions for the rest and invite correction.

697
00:36:32.283 --> 00:36:33.329
Question 3.

698
00:36:33.609 --> 00:36:35.742
How precise do estimates need to be?

699
00:36:36.022 --> 00:36:37.387
Thirty-second answer:

700
00:36:37.667 --> 00:36:49.614
Precise enough not to change decisions: one significant figure per input, standard shortcuts (a day about 10⁵ s), and explicit thresholds; flag results near a threshold.

701
00:36:50.964 --> 00:36:52.031
Question 4.

702
00:36:52.311 --> 00:36:54.359
How do you choose what to deep dive on?

703
00:36:54.639 --> 00:36:56.004
Thirty-second answer:

704
00:36:56.284 --> 00:37:06.055
Where scarcity, money, concurrency or extreme scale meet, the part most likely to break or be wrong, or what the interviewer steers to; propose it and confirm.

705
00:37:07.405 --> 00:37:08.407
Intermediate.

706
00:37:08.687 --> 00:37:09.797
Question 5.

707
00:37:10.077 --> 00:37:12.381
How do you justify a technology choice?

708
00:37:12.661 --> 00:37:14.026
Thirty-second answer:

709
00:37:14.306 --> 00:37:20.727
Tie it to requirements and numbers, state its costs, and name the condition under which you'd choose the alternative:

710
00:37:21.007 --> 00:37:32.122
Kafka because we need replay and per-key ordering at 50k per second; it costs operational complexity; for 1k per second without replay, a database queue would do.

711
00:37:33.472 --> 00:37:34.581
Question 6.

712
00:37:34.861 --> 00:37:37.357
What if you realise mid-interview that you went wrong?

713
00:37:37.637 --> 00:37:39.003
Thirty-second answer:

714
00:37:39.283 --> 00:37:47.603
Say so explicitly, explain what changed your mind, and adjust, noticing and correcting is a positive signal; hiding it is not.

715
00:37:48.953 --> 00:37:50.041
Question 7.

716
00:37:50.321 --> 00:37:52.518
How much of the design should be on the board?

717
00:37:52.798 --> 00:37:54.163
Thirty-second answer:

718
00:37:54.443 --> 00:38:05.601
Requirements, key numbers, a clear diagram with the critical path and sync or async edges, the data model's keys, and a short failure table, the interviewer's write-up follows the board.

719
00:38:06.951 --> 00:38:07.975
Question 8.

720
00:38:08.255 --> 00:38:10.260
How do you handle a domain you don't know?

721
00:38:10.540 --> 00:38:11.905
Thirty-second answer:

722
00:38:12.185 --> 00:38:24.687
Use the framework, requirements and numbers are domain-independent; ask domain questions as requirements; map to known patterns (booking, feed, ledger, matching); be explicit about assumptions.

723
00:38:26.037 --> 00:38:27.103
Question 9.

724
00:38:27.383 --> 00:38:29.261
How do you avoid over-engineering?

725
00:38:29.541 --> 00:38:30.906
Thirty-second answer:

726
00:38:31.186 --> 00:38:43.943
Start with the simplest design that meets the requirements and add each component only when a requirement or number demands it, saying which; mention the evolution path for 10 times instead of building it now.

727
00:38:45.293 --> 00:38:46.061
Senior.

728
00:38:46.341 --> 00:38:47.387
Question 10.

729
00:38:47.667 --> 00:38:50.803
What distinguishes a senior answer from a mid-level one?

730
00:38:51.083 --> 00:38:52.448
Thirty-second answer:

731
00:38:52.728 --> 00:39:08.941
Completeness and correctness under realistic constraints: explicit requirements and numbers, a coherent design, one deep dive with code-level precision (atomic operations, state machines), named trade-offs, and failure handling on the critical path.

732
00:39:10.291 --> 00:39:11.422
Question 11.

733
00:39:11.702 --> 00:39:14.433
How do you discuss failures without running out of time?

734
00:39:14.713 --> 00:39:16.078
Thirty-second answer:

735
00:39:16.358 --> 00:39:29.691
A compact failure table for the critical path, component, failure, detection, user impact, recovery, plus money or inventory specifics (unknown outcomes, idempotency, sagas); 5 to 6 minutes.

736
00:39:31.041 --> 00:39:32.129
Question 12.

737
00:39:32.409 --> 00:39:34.543
How do you present data model choices?

738
00:39:34.823 --> 00:39:36.188
Thirty-second answer:

739
00:39:36.468 --> 00:39:48.287
From access patterns: list the queries and writes, pick keys or partitioning for them, choose the store per pattern with a reason, and note consistency needs (transactions for money or stock).

740
00:39:49.637 --> 00:39:50.917
Question 13.

741
00:39:51.197 --> 00:39:52.989
How do you use the interviewer's hints?

742
00:39:53.269 --> 00:39:54.634
Thirty-second answer:

743
00:39:54.914 --> 00:40:05.047
Treat every question as steering: acknowledge, answer briefly, and adjust the plan, a question about X usually means they want depth on X or think you missed it.

744
00:40:06.397 --> 00:40:07.592
Question 14.

745
00:40:07.872 --> 00:40:09.920
How should you prepare in the last two weeks?

746
00:40:10.200 --> 00:40:11.565
Thirty-second answer:

747
00:40:11.845 --> 00:40:25.605
Timed practice of 8 to 10 core prompts on paper before reading solutions, mock interviews scored against a rubric, a personal list of thresholds and shortcuts, and 2 to 3 stories of real systems you can deep dive on.

748
00:40:26.955 --> 00:40:27.851
Architect.

749
00:40:28.131 --> 00:40:29.326
Question 15.

750
00:40:29.606 --> 00:40:32.123
What distinguishes a staff or principal answer?

751
00:40:32.403 --> 00:40:33.769
Thirty-second answer:

752
00:40:34.049 --> 00:40:50.006
Driving ambiguity (scoping, phasing), trade-offs argued with flip conditions, failure handling and operability as first-class design (blast radius, SLOs, cost), organisational awareness (teams, migration), and knowing what not to build.

753
00:40:51.356 --> 00:40:52.551
Question 16.

754
00:40:52.831 --> 00:40:55.519
How do you run a 'design your past system' interview?

755
00:40:55.799 --> 00:40:57.164
Thirty-second answer:

756
00:40:57.444 --> 00:41:08.836
Context and constraints, the key decisions with alternatives considered, what went wrong and how you found out, metrics of impact, and what you would change now, owning mistakes and showing learning.

757
00:41:10.186 --> 00:41:11.530
Question 17.

758
00:41:11.810 --> 00:41:14.989
How do you handle an interviewer who challenges a correct choice?

759
00:41:15.269 --> 00:41:16.634
Thirty-second answer:

760
00:41:16.914 --> 00:41:31.719
Engage with their criterion, ask what they're weighing, restate your reasoning with the requirement it serves, concede where their point changes the balance, and state the flip condition; it's a test of reasoning, not of stubbornness or capitulation.

761
00:41:33.069 --> 00:41:34.243
Question 18.

762
00:41:34.523 --> 00:41:37.000
How do you bring cost and operations into a design interview?

763
00:41:38.280 --> 00:41:39.645
Thirty-second answer:

764
00:41:39.925 --> 00:41:54.923
A sentence per major component on cost drivers (egress, storage, compute at peak), unit cost (per order per request), the operability plan (SLOs, dashboards, on-call), and where you'd accept degraded behaviour to save money.

765
00:41:56.273 --> 00:41:57.510
Question 19.

766
00:41:57.790 --> 00:41:59.966
How do you show organisational thinking?

767
00:42:00.246 --> 00:42:01.611
Thirty-second answer:

768
00:42:01.891 --> 00:42:15.587
Mention team boundaries aligned with services (Conway), migration strategies (strangler, dual writes), rollout plans (flags, canaries), and governance (ADRs, SLO ownership) where they affect the design.

769
00:42:16.937 --> 00:42:18.047
Question 20.

770
00:42:18.327 --> 00:42:22.508
What are the most common reasons strong engineers fail design interviews?

771
00:42:22.788 --> 00:42:24.153
Thirty-second answer:

772
00:42:24.433 --> 00:42:38.172
Designing their own past system instead of the prompt, skipping requirements and numbers, no deep dive, defending choices by authority, and running out of time before failures, all fixable with the framework and timed practice.

773
00:42:39.522 --> 00:42:40.418
Part 9.

774
00:42:40.698 --> 00:42:42.106
Summary and Practice.

775
00:42:43.356 --> 00:42:44.444
Key takeaways.

776
00:42:44.724 --> 00:42:45.897
Clarify first.

777
00:42:46.177 --> 00:42:48.140
Scale and latency split the space.

778
00:42:48.420 --> 00:42:50.703
State the rest as assumptions (L3).

779
00:42:50.983 --> 00:42:53.159
Estimate to one significant figure.

780
00:42:53.439 --> 00:42:55.167
Name the thresholds (L1).

781
00:42:55.447 --> 00:42:59.820
Trade-offs: know robust from close calls, and the flip conditions (L2).

782
00:43:00.100 --> 00:43:02.020
Win in the deep dive and failures.

783
00:43:02.300 --> 00:43:04.412
That's where the rubric weights are (L4).

784
00:43:04.692 --> 00:43:07.657
Timebox out loud and keep the board as the record.

